-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
                       FIFO_DEPTH=16          FIFO_DEPTH=64          FIFO_DEPTH=256
SIZE(B)  LATENCY  |  PEAK  FULL?  |  PEAK  FULL?  |  PEAK  FULL?
     64       94  |     6    no  |     6    no   |     6    no
    128      157  |    12    no  |    12    no   |    12    no
    256      285  |    16   YES  |    24    no   |    24    no
    512      541  |    16   YES  |    48    no   |    48    no
   1024     1053  |    16   YES  |    64   YES   |    96    no
   4096     4125  |    16   YES  |    64   YES   |   256   YES

Key result: LATENCY is identical across all three FIFO_DEPTH values for
every packet size. This is correct, expected behavior, not a flaw in the
experiment:

  Once the sink's fixed drain rate (1-in-4 cycles) becomes the pipeline
  bottleneck, the total time to push a fixed amount of data through it is
  set by that rate alone (~4 cycles/beat), independent of how much buffer
  sits upstream. A deeper FIFO only delays *when* back-pressure starts
  reaching the producer — it cannot make the consumer drain faster. This
  is a direct consequence of queueing at a single fixed-rate server: buffer
  depth changes transient/burst absorption, not steady-state throughput of
  a single continuous flow.

  What FIFO_DEPTH *does* change, exactly as the depth-sweep experiment is
  documented to measure (see Readme.md "axis_fifo" section): how much of
  the packet the FIFO can absorb before fifo_full asserts and back-pressure
  ripples upstream.
    - DEPTH=16  (128 B) fills for every packet >= 256 B — matches "stalls
      very quickly for large packets."
    - DEPTH=64  (512 B) fills only for packets >= 1 KB — matches "stalls
      only for multi-KB bursts."
    - DEPTH=256 (2 KB)  fills only for the 4 KB packet, since even a 2 KB
      buffer is smaller than a 4 KB payload — matches "rarely stalls."
  This is the correct, useful FIFO_DEPTH finding: it governs buffering
  headroom / how early back-pressure engages, not single-flow completion
  latency under a sustained bottleneck. To make FIFO_DEPTH move the total
  latency number itself, the workload would need to be burst + idle-gap
  traffic (so a deeper buffer lets the producer get further ahead between
  bursts) rather than one continuous packet driven flat-out — that is a
  different experiment, not implemented here.

